or modified the Fax service's binary path to point to PowerShell via sc.exe, then started the service to achieve code execution with NETWORK SERVICE privileges. This is service modification for persistence/privilege escalation, not merely service execution\u2014the registry ImagePath was permanently altered to redirect the service executable.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E010","field":"TargetObject","quote":"HKLM\\System\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Fax\\ImagePath","ok":true},{"event_id":"E006","field":"CommandLine","quote":"sc config Fax binPath= \"C:\\windows\\system32\\WindowsPowerShell","ok":true},{"event_id":"E020","field":"User","quote":"NT AUTHORITY\\NETWORK SERVICE","ok":true}],"attempts":1},{"technique_id":"T1012","name":"Query Registry","parent":null,"tactics":["Discovery"],"behavior":"PowerShell running as NETWORK SERVICE accessed sensitive registry keys including LSA and Winlogon configuration, likely for credential enumeration or system reconnaissance.","slot":1,"reasoning":"The evidence shows PowerShell querying LSA and Winlogon registry keys with read-only access masks (0x1 and 0x20019), which is consistent with system reconnaissance and enumeration rather than credential dumping or persistence modification. The access pattern\u2014opening handles and reading from sensitive configuration keys\u2014matches Query Registry behavior used for gathering system information.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E023","field":"ObjectName","quote":"\\REGISTRY\\MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\ControlSet001\\Control\\Lsa","ok":true},{"event_id":"E024","field":"AccessMask","quote":"0x1","ok":true},{"event_id":"E041","field":"ObjectName","quote":"\\REGISTRY\\MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon","ok":true}],"attempts":1},{"technique_id":"T1685.005","name":"Clear Windows Event Logs","parent":"Disable or Modify Tools","tactics":["Defense Impairment"],"behavior":"The System event log was cleared at the start of the recording, and the Fax service binary path was later restored to its legitimate value, indicating anti-forensics and cleanup.","slot":2,"reasoning":"The primary adversary objective here is clearing the System event log to remove evidence of intrusion activity, which is indicator removal for anti-forensics purposes. While the Fax service binary path restoration appears to be cleanup of a prior modification, the event log clearing is the core defensive evasion technique being demonstrated.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E001","field":"Channel","quote":"System","ok":true},{"event_id":"E047","field":"CommandLine","quote":"sc config Fax binPath= \"C:\\WINDOWS\\system32\\fxssvc.exe\"","ok":true}],"attempts":1}],"unverified":[],"declined":[],"hypotheses":[{"behavior":"User wardog modified the Fax service binary path via sc.exe to execute PowerShell, then started the service to run code with NETWORK SERVICE privileges.","event_ids":["E006","E007","E010","E014","E020"],"keywords":["sc config","binPath","Windows Service","T1543.003","service hijacking","privilege escalation"]},{"behavior":"PowerShell running as NETWORK SERVICE accessed sensitive registry keys including LSA and Winlogon configuration, likely for credential enumeration or system reconnaissance.","event_ids":["E023","E024","E040","E041"],"keywords":["registry access","LSA","Winlogon","credential access","system enumeration","NETWORK SERVICE"]},{"behavior":"The System event log was cleared at the start of the recording, and the Fax service binary path was later restored to its legitimate value, indicating anti-forensics and cleanup.","event_ids":["E001","E047","E049"],"keywords":["event log clearing","anti-forensics","log deletion","cleanup","service restoration"]}],"retried":[]},"trace":[{"t":1.93,"agent":"hunter","kind":"text","text":"I'll analyze this alert systematically, starting with the triage view to identify the adversary's objectives."},{"t":1.93,"agent":"hunter","kind":"tool_result","tool":"search_events","result":{"matches":5,"events":"<log_events trust=\"